model {
  extend basalt.modelGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "The runtime adapter may end the single pool controller instance at any time and asks callers to reconnect. The model gateway entry treats that closed error class, never an overloaded one, as a controller reset. The controller may already have dispatched any request it received, so the entry replays only a safe read that writes no request record, account choice or usage: GET or HEAD without an upgrade, excluding video status polls. It waits one second, re-resolves the controller and sends once more; a second reset returns retry advice. A model call that failed before its response gets the native retry advice with Retry-After of one second: overloaded status 529 for messages and a server error 503 for other model routes. Its client may then resend a call the old instance had already dispatched; that is a separate, separately recorded attempt, the same trade the fleet makes after a dispatched runtime crash, and it keeps the turn alive. A second reset of a control read gets the backend-unavailable 503. A control write keeps its existing unknown-outcome failure, because its callers may not assume it was not applied. A started stream is never replayed. A messages or responses event stream ends with the native retry terminal; other started bodies and model sockets are still cut and are not recorded at this hop. A reader that leaves is not an interruption. Each event writes one closed POOL_CONTROLLER_INTERRUPTED line with count 1, outcome, cause, method and endpoint template, and one pool_controller_interrupted observability adapter event joined by request identifier. Exception text, query strings and bodies never enter either."
    }
  }
}
